WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.780 --> 00:00:03.360
Hi, I'm Sandra, from HEARa. 

2
00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:07.559
Welcome to Day 3 of our 10 
day Lipreading Challenge. 

3
00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:12.540
designed to help you develop and 
practice your lip reading skills! 

4
00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:15.600
Hearing loss can have a 
profound impact on your life. 

5
00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:19.860
It touches every interaction.
It can affect your career, 

6
00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:22.320
your relationships,
and your happiness. 

7
00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:27.599
Join me to explore tips and 
tricks on this YouTube channel 

8
00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:33.240
to bring confidence and clarity to relationships
in all areas of your life. 

9
00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:38.220
Today we're moving on to 
a new group of homophenes. 

10
00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:40.140
YES!!

11
00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:42.899
But before we do that, what's a homophene again? 

12
00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:48.060
Homophenes are groups of sounds 
that look alike on the lips. 

13
00:00:48.060 --> 00:00:51.480
If you haven't already watched the 
first two days of the challenge, 

14
00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:53.600
I recommend starting there.

15
00:00:53.601 --> 00:00:56.700
The F and V sounds are homophenes.

16
00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:01.980
To make these sounds, your upper 
teeth come to your lower lip. 

17
00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:03.540
Watch:

18
00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:04.559
belief

19
00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:06.500
believe

20
00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:10.560
These two words look exactly the same,

21
00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:14.309
even though they don't sound the same at all.

22
00:01:14.309 --> 00:01:17.187
It is possible to confuse these two words,

23
00:01:17.188 --> 00:01:22.033
because the only difference between them is just a fraction of a second

24
00:01:22.033 --> 00:01:24.201
of vibration of your vocal cords.

25
00:01:24.201 --> 00:01:28.037
If your attention is elsewhere for that fraction of a second,

26
00:01:28.038 --> 00:01:32.612
or if there's some background noise covering up a word or two,

27
00:01:32.612 --> 00:01:34.619
you could confuse these two words.

28
00:01:34.760 --> 00:01:41.780
That's why substitution practice is important.
We do this so that we can quickly switch gears  

29
00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:46.079
when the first thing we see 
or hear does not make sense. 

30
00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:54.299
Substitution practice is a good exercise
because it gets your mind in that switching mode 

31
00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:57.709
where you're substituting one sound for another.

32
00:01:57.709 --> 00:02:00.060
That helps us to stay flexible.

33
00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:09.179
In our substitution practice today,
I'm going to say a sentence with an F or V error 

34
00:02:09.180 --> 00:02:14.519
and I want you to make the substitution:
an F for V, or vice versa. 

35
00:02:15.780 --> 00:02:20.819
As usual the answers are written in 
the section below the video frame. 

36
00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:23.827
Are you ready?

37
00:02:24.703 --> 00:02:29.727
Number 1: Sugar is the new vat.

38
00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:37.526
Number 2: It's hot in here, turn on the van.

39
00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:46.140
Number 3: That grape fine is 
taking over the backyard. 

40
00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:53.581
Number 4: There are many vicious in the sea.

41
00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:59.880
Are you finding it way easier when there's 
only two sounds in the homophene group? 

42
00:03:00.420 --> 00:03:03.699
Versus three in day 1.

43
00:03:03.699 --> 00:03:06.540
Let's amp up the difficulty a little bit.

44
00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:14.208
Number 5: He apologized to no a fail.

45
00:03:14.208 --> 00:03:20.691
Number 6: I am alone in pie believe.

46
00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:28.595
Number 7: We will have to hire 
a van to poof the furniture. 

47
00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:35.178
Number 8: The tennis player won his surf.

48
00:03:35.178 --> 00:03:42.000
Number 9: I went skiing in fail this year.

49
00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:51.120
Number 10: I don't like the 
saying, Appy wife, appy live. 

50
00:03:51.900 --> 00:03:54.161
H is invisible, remember?

51
00:03:54.521 --> 00:03:57.899
That's it for our substitution practice today,

52
00:03:57.899 --> 00:04:02.460
but I wanted to talk a little bit 
about flexibility before you go.

53
00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:05.961
Someone once said about lip reading:

54
00:04:05.961 --> 00:04:06.690
I'm stubborn.

55
00:04:06.690 --> 00:04:11.754
If I think I've seen a word, I 
hang on to it like a terrier.

56
00:04:11.754 --> 00:04:17.392
Sometimes we have to let go of the 
first thought that comes to mind

57
00:04:17.392 --> 00:04:22.200
to find a better word or phrase 
with a similar lip shape.

58
00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:27.181
This is one of the hallmarks of a good lip reader,

59
00:04:27.181 --> 00:04:28.920
this ability to let go.

60
00:04:30.660 --> 00:04:33.900
When we get really good at this,
we can do it in real time 

61
00:04:33.900 --> 00:04:38.100
to make that quick substitution
and find something else that makes sense. 

62
00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:42.720
Yesterday, in day 2 I said about context: 

63
00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:47.640
when you know what the context is,
you can anticipate what follows. 

64
00:04:48.840 --> 00:04:52.560
Except when you're wrong 
about what the context is! 

65
00:04:52.560 --> 00:04:57.720
So for example,
you see and hear the word flower, 

66
00:04:59.220 --> 00:05:03.660
but if what follows is about 
wheat free and chickpea flour, 

67
00:05:03.660 --> 00:05:06.780
then you're going to have 
to drop your anticipation 

68
00:05:06.780 --> 00:05:14.340
of wildflowers and flowery meadows
and start to tune in to the concept of baking. 

69
00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:20.159
I'd like to show you how this could 
make the conversation go sideways. 

70
00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:26.700
Let's say I said the word vlog.
There was some background noise,  

71
00:05:26.700 --> 00:05:29.040
so you didn't hear it, but you did see it. 

72
00:05:29.880 --> 00:05:34.475
A vlog is a blog in the medium of video.

73
00:05:34.475 --> 00:05:39.300
A word that looks similar to vlog is flaw,

74
00:05:40.080 --> 00:05:42.240
because the G is invisible.

75
00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:43.259
Watch:

76
00:05:44.220 --> 00:05:45.000
vlog

77
00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:47.000
flaw.

78
00:05:48.720 --> 00:05:51.180
I'm going to work on my vlog. 

79
00:05:52.920 --> 00:05:56.133
You only have one flaw?

80
00:05:56.133 --> 00:05:57.960
Are you asking me or telling me?

81
00:05:59.340 --> 00:05:59.840
What??

82
00:06:00.577 --> 00:06:05.454
Remember to pay attention to any mismatch

83
00:06:05.454 --> 00:06:08.203
between what you thought
you saw

84
00:06:08.203 --> 00:06:10.875
and facial expression and body language.

85
00:06:10.875 --> 00:06:14.474
If the other person is 
frowning or looking confused,

86
00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:18.812
then you may have misunderstood.

87
00:06:18.812 --> 00:06:22.189
Stay flexible and clarify what you thought you saw.

88
00:06:22.189 --> 00:06:23.580
That's all for today.

89
00:06:24.360 --> 00:06:25.969
Thank you for joining me.

90
00:06:25.969 --> 00:06:27.900
I hope you found this helpful.

91
00:06:28.680 --> 00:06:34.560
Tomorrow, we'll get some practice
lip reading F and V sounds in sentences. 

92
00:06:35.820 --> 00:06:40.320
In the meantime, please like my video,
subscribe to my channel, 

93
00:06:40.320 --> 00:06:42.220
and share this with others.

94
00:06:42.220 --> 00:06:43.980
I'd really appreciate it.

95
00:06:45.120 --> 00:06:47.639
Pie!
I mean, Bye!